/* Modern theme's shared window shell -- the "metal rail" chrome (base.rcss's .modern-frame/
   .modern-shell-edge/.modern-groove/.modern-header-rail/.modern-content-well family), factored out
   of generic_menu_dialog.rcss into its own template (window_shell.rml) so any consumer -- dialog or
   plain window -- can reuse the same markup instead of hand-copying it. Loaded automatically by any
   document that links window_shell.rml as a template (RmlUi merges a template's own <head> links
   into the consuming document's head) -- a consumer does not need its own <link> to this file.

   Named `window-shell-*`, not `gmd-*` -- this markup is no longer generic-menu-dialog-specific (see
   window_shell.rml's own comment). Values below are unchanged from generic_menu_dialog.rcss's
   former .gmd-content-well rule, just renamed. */
.window-shell-content {
	left: 14dp;
	right: 14dp;
	top: 10dp;
	bottom: 16dp;
	pointer-events: auto;
	display: flex;
	flex-direction: column;
	justify-content: center;
	align-items: center;
}
/* With a title, the header rail (base.rcss's .modern-header-rail, top:7dp height:38dp) visually
   overlaps this well's own top edge -- painting order alone (well before rail in the .rml) already
   keeps the rail's opaque fill on top there, but the well's own FLEX CONTENT still needs to clear
   the rail's bottom edge, hence this padding rather than moving the well's own box. justify-content
   also switches to flex-start here -- centering the remainder under the banner would produce a
   large, content-dependent gap above the first line/button; untitled consumers keep centering since
   they have no banner to compete with. */
.window-shell-content.has-title {
	padding-top: 40dp;
	justify-content: flex-start;
}
/* .window-shell-header-rail needs no rule of its own -- it's co-applied with base.rcss's own
   .modern-header-rail, which already supplies the full look (position/size/decorator/box-shadow).
   The window-shell- class exists only so this element has a name that isn't tied to any one
   consumer, for anything a future variant might need to target specifically. */
